     "body": "Came up while going through the size-and-time caches next to the gateway after #3708. A JS gateway plugin's module can import files beside it (`jsmod.Compile` reads them), but `stamp()` in internal/middleware/middleware.go only has plugins.json, plugins/package.json and bun.lock, and each middleware's entry file. So when only a file the entry imports changes, the middleware is never loaded again: the old code keeps running until the entry or plugins.json changes, or magpie restarts. A fix to an import that didn't compile is missed the same way, so the Plugins page keeps showing the old load error.\n\nThe docs say it should be picked up: \"magpie looks at the files and plugins.json once a second, and loads a middleware again when they changed\" (site/public/docs/plugins.html), and the Reload line in docs/subsystems/gateway-middleware.md.\n\nOn 7a60e78c: a folder plugin whose index.js imports a model map from ./names.js. After names.js is rewritten (another size, a later time), requests still get the old mapping. internal/agentplug's `stamp()` has the same shape: an agent module whose `name` comes from ./name.js still has the old name after name.js is edited.\n\nIt only bites someone working on a plugin of their own added from a folder. A package from npm or git is replaced whole on update, and that changes bun.lock, which is stamped.\n\nI'll take it. The plan: `jsmod.Compile` also gives back the files it imported, each with its time and size from just before it was read, and both loaders load again when one of them changed.\n",
